Why this comment block sits *after* the `` line, unlike every other template in this codebase (which opens with its docstring comment): the XML declaration is only well-formed as the very first thing in the document -- no whitespace, no comment, nothing at all may precede it (an XML parser rejects even a single leading blank line, see this feature's report for the failure this project hit and fixed while building it). This comment block itself renders to nothing, but the newline immediately following its own closing tag does not disappear -- so placing it *before* `` would leave exactly that one stray leading newline in the response body. Placing it here instead, between the declaration and the root `` element, is still fine: that gap is `Misc` space in XML's own grammar (comments/whitespace are explicitly allowed between the prolog and the root element), and it's the only position in this file that is both after the mandatory declaration and before the root element.
(Note for future editors of this comment: never quote this tag's own opening/closing delimiters verbatim inside its own body -- Django's comment-tag scanner matches on the raw token stream, not nested comment-awareness, so writing out the literal closing-tag text in here would terminate this block early and dump the rest of this comment's prose straight into the compiled template as if it were real markup.)
Template contract (from `apps.planner.export.build_svg_export()` -- load-bearing, mirrors `apps/planner/grid.py`'s own "compute everything server-side, templates only iterate" precedent, PRD R2): `svg_export` (an `apps.planner.export.SvgExport`) arrives with every position, size, and color already resolved to plain pixel integers / hex-color strings. This template performs zero math and makes zero layout decisions -- it only loops over `svg_export.rects` then `svg_export.texts` and emits one ``/`` element per entry, in that order (rects first so every text element paints on top of its own rect, never underneath it).
Escaping: Django's autoescaping is on by default project-wide (confirmed directly against `config/settings.py` -- `TEMPLATES[0]['OPTIONS']` sets no `autoescape: False`, and no `{% autoescape off %}` block appears anywhere in this file), so every `{{ }}` variable below -- most importantly `text.content`, which carries a user's own free-text block label straight into XML markup -- is escaped before being written out. Django's escaping converts `&`, `<`, `>` (plus `'`/`"`) to their entity equivalents, which is exactly what turns an untrusted label like `Q&A ` into well-formed XML text content (`Q&A <review>`) instead of a parser error or a markup-injection vector. Do not wrap any part of this file in `{% autoescape off %}` or pipe any variable through `|safe` -- a block label is user-supplied, untrusted content, and turning escaping off here would let it break the document's own XML well-formedness (a stray `<` or `&` is fatal to an XML parser, unlike an HTML parser's much more forgiving error recovery).
